The flux at 1 keV at 10^{2} days (1.4 nJy) is larger than the flux at 9 days by a factor of \\sim5, implying that \\alpha\\sim 0.7. We thus conclude from equation (3) that the energy in the shocked plasma increased by a factor \\sim20 between these two epochs. There are two ways that the energy in the external shock could increase with time. One is that the compact remnant left over in the merger continues to pump energy into the outward moving shock front. The other is that energy is supplied to the part of the external shock wave that we see by slower moving ejecta or a structured jet2.

